    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0328, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7997\n\nSPONSOR: BOYLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to commercial fishing licenses\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend and modify the eligibility limita-\ntions for commercial fishing licenses and permits\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend Environmental Conservation Law (ECL)\n§ 13-0328 to: 1) maintain a cap on the number of commercial food fish,\ncrab, whelk (conch), and Marine and Coastal District Party and Charter\nBoat licenses or permits that may be issued; 2) maintain the current\nmoratorium on new lobster permits; and 3) maintain the current eligibil-\nity requirements.\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that the bill shall take effect December\n31, 2018, provided that any regulations necessary for the timely imple-\nmentation of the provisions of this act on its effective date may be\npromulgated on or before such date.\n\n \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nSince ECL § 13-0328 was last amended in 2017, the number of commercial\nfishing licenses and permits issued has generally continued to decline\nas follows:\n\n                        2015       2016      2017\n\nResident Food Fish      932        928       924\nNon-Resident Food Fish  41         41        40\nResident Crab           541        538       537\nNon-Resident Crab       25         26        25\nResident Lobster        293        280       273\nNon-Resident Lobster    18         18        18\nResident Whelk          239        241       240\nNon-Resident Whelk      10         11        11\nParty & Charter Boat    488        492       508\n\nThis bill would maintain the current license and permit numbers. The\nmaximum number of party and charter boat licenses that may be issued to\nuninspected passenger vessels would remain at 517. Further, a complete\nmoratorium would be maintained on commercial lobster permits, given that\nsignificant excess harvesting capacity exists in the lobster fishery,\nand current lobster management regulations do not provide for issuance\nof the required lobster trap tags to any new persons. The substantial\ndecline in lobster abundance that has resulted from episodes of mortal-\nity beginning in 1999 also supports continuing to prevent new entrants\nin this troubled fishery.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nECL § 13-0328 was last amended to extend and modify its provisions in\nChapter 225 of the Laws of 2017.\n\n \nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nA continuing reduction in the number of commercial fishing licenses and\npermits issued by DEC would result in declining revenue to the Marine\nResources Account of the Conservation Fund.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act would take effect immediately.\n",
